  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nPersistent northwest flow has certainly made it feel like fall\nso far today with latest obs reporting temperatures merely in\nthe upper 40s to low 60s. Dry air advection within this same \nregime has been enhanced throughout the day not only by an \neastward- expanding area of high pressure, but downsloping \neffects of the northwesterly wind. Therefore, not only is it \nchilly, it's also rather dry with dewpoints currently in the \nupper 20s to low 30s. Low humidity paired with breezy conditions\namid a slackening pressure gradient has made it quite crisp for\nearly October. \n\nThe aforementioned high will move overhead later this evening,\nallowing skies to remain clear through most of the overnight. A\nweak warm front will begin to approach the region before\ndaybreak tomorrow, potentially allowing for some high-level\nclouds to encroach on the Adirondacks. However, these will\nlikely not move in soon enough nor will they be thick enough to\nchallenge the maximum potential of radiational cooling.\nEspecially since forecast soundings show a very clear-cut sign\nof decoupling which will allow winds to go calm. Therefore, low\ntemperatures tonight are expected to drop to the upper 20s to\nmid 30s. For areas where the growing season is ongoing (i.e.\nportions of the Upper Hudson Valley, Capital District, Mid-\nHudson Valley, southern Taconics, and Litchfield County), this\nwill pose a detriment to tender and sensitive vegetation.\nTherefore, Freeze Warnings and Frost Advisories were maintained.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe cold front closely attending tomorrow morning's warm front \ncould bring some isolated rain showers to portions of the \nSouthern Adirondacks and western Mohawk Valley tomorrow into \nThursday. However, that will likely be our only bout of\nprecipitation through as early as Sunday night as high pressure\nlargely dominates throughout the rest of the week and into the\nweekend. And while guidance is in agreement that an area of rain\nwill move into the region from southwest to northeast beginning\nSunday night into Monday, there remains uncertainty in the track\nof the resulting low. At this point, there are no hydrological\nconcerns resulting from this system as, by that point, we will\nbe in need of the rain.",
